<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Fandane-Thiouthioune</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Somb</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Fandane-Thiouthioune, also called the Battle of Somb, was fought on 18 July 1867 in the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> army of Maad a Sinig Kumba Ndoffene Famak Joof defeated the forces of the Muslim reformer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maba-diakhou-ba">Maba Diakhou Ba</a>, who was killed in the battle. The victory ended Maba's holy war against the Serer kingdoms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fandane-thiouthioune.jpg" alt="Battle of Fandane-Thiouthioune" width="320" height="428" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tamsier · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Serer_Royal_War_Drum_(Jung-Jung)._19th_Century._Jung-Jung_From_The_Kingdom_of_Sine_(in_modern_day_Senegal).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war grew out of the ambitions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lat-dior">Lat Dior</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cayor">Cayor</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baol">Baol</a>. Defeated by the French at the Battle of Loro on 12 January 1864 and exiled, he took refuge with the Maad a Sinig, who refused to lend him troops because he was himself in dispute with the French over Joal, one of his provinces. Lat Dior then turned to Maba, the Almami of Nioro du Rip, who promised to help him regain his throne if he converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Lat Dior converted and in 1867 proposed a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a>.</p><p>In April 1867 the marabouts surprised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> village of Mbin o Ngor. They were driven back, but Boucary Ngoneh Joof, a relative and counsellor of the king, was killed, and the king's daughter Lingeer Selbeh Ndoffene Joof was abducted and forcibly married to Maba's brother Abdoulaye Ouli Ba. The Maad a Sinig wrote to Maba challenging him to meet the full army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> in open battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Maba came with arms and ammunition obtained in British Gambia, accompanied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lat-dior">Lat Dior</a>, the Bourba Jolof Alboury Sainabou Njie and other allies. According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> oral tradition the king's Jaraff, Wassaly Sene, performed the ancestral rites, raised his spear to the heavens, struck it into the ground and foretold victory. Fighting began at Somb in heavy rain; the marabouts broke into Thiouthioune and killed several Serer princes, and the prince Mbagne Somb Faye killed himself when captured.</p><p>Around midday Maba's men sheltered in the coppices round the pond of Fandane. When the king arrived with the armies of Patar and Poukham, cutting the marabouts' road home through Saloum, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lat-dior">Lat Dior</a> and Alboury abandoned Maba and fought their way out. Maba was killed at Fandane, as was the prince Makhoureja Ngoneh Joof; the king was wounded, and Maba's general Mama Gaolo Nyang was taken prisoner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The king had Maba's body dismembered and sent the head and an arm with a letter to the French commandant at Gorée. The French administration under Governor Émile Pinet-Laprade had armed neither side, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> refused it more troops. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-sine">Sine</a> still chant a victory hymn in the king's honour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>'s Ministry of Culture states that Maba is buried in the mausoleum of Mbel Fandane, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serer">Serer</a> tradition holds that the tomb is that of Makhoureja Ngoneh Joof.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Fandane-Thiouthioune” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maba Diakhou Bâ, considered a national hero and given saint-like status by Senegambian Muslims, was killed in battle against the Serer king of Sine, Kumba Ndoffene Famak Joof, on 18 July 1867 at the <b>Battle of Fandane-Thiouthioune</b>, commonly called the Battle of Somb.</blockquote><a href="/religion/serer-religion">Serer religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1867 its army defeated and killed the jihadist Maba Diakhou Ba at <b>Fandane-Thiouthioune</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-sine">Kingdom of Sine</a></li></ul></section>
